 ### Seam Ownership
-Seam Ownership is an architectural method for chunk meshing where each chunk meshes only its interior voxels, and a single seam job is assigned to each chunk boundary to read both adjacent sides' voxels, preventing inter-chunk waiting or re-meshing [S3, unofficial]. The process follows four steps: 1. Mesh each chunk's interior voxels independently. 2. Assign exactly one seam job per chunk boundary. 3. Seam job reads voxel data from both adjacent chunks. 4. Seam job generates boundary geometry without blocking or triggering neighbour re-meshing [S3, unofficial]. This approach ensures that a chunk never waits for, or re-meshes on, a neighbor, improving parallelism [S3, unofficial]. The method has been used in cubic meshing since world modes review M5 [S3, unofficial].
+Seam Ownership is an architectural method for chunk meshing where each chunk meshes only its interior, and a single seam job is assigned to each chunk boundary to read both adjacent sides' voxels, preventing inter-chunk waiting or re-meshing [S3, unofficial]. The process follows four steps: 1. Mesh each chunk's interior voxels independently [S3, unofficial]. 2. Assign exactly one seam job per chunk boundary [S3, unofficial]. 3. Seam job reads voxel data from both adjacent chunks [S3, unofficial]. 4. Seam job generates boundary geometry without blocking or triggering neighbour re-meshing [S3, unofficial]. This approach ensures that a chunk never waits for, or re-meshes on, a neighbor, improving parallelism [S3, unofficial]. The method has been used in cubic meshing since world modes review M5 [S3, unofficial].
 
